# 3. APPLICATION ARCHITECTURE

Use a modular monolithic architecture.

The application should initially be deployed as one Vercel project.

Suggested domains:

- brosdev.info — Main marketing website
- app.brosdev.info — Customer dashboard
- api.brosdev.info — Main REST API
- forms.brosdev.info — Form submission endpoints
- files.brosdev.info — File delivery
- docs.brosdev.info — API documentation
- hooks.brosdev.info — Webhook endpoints

These domains may route to the same Vercel deployment initially.

Keep the application organized into independent modules so services can be separated later if required.

Architecture:

Customer Website
       |
       v
BrosDev APIs / HTML Integrations
       |
       v
Vercel Backend
       |
       +---- Authentication
       |
       +---- Project Management
       |
       +---- Forms Service
       |
       +---- Email Service
       |
       +---- Webhook Service
       |
       +---- Newsletter Service
       |
       +---- File Service
       |
       +---- Billing Service
       |
       v
Firebase Firestore
       |
       +---- Firebase Authentication
       |
       +---- Firebase Storage
       |
       v
External Services
       |
       +---- Resend
       +---- Razorpay

Important Vercel requirements:

- Use Vercel-compatible serverless functions.
- Adapt Express routing to serverless execution.
- Do not depend on persistent local files.
- Do not assume a process remains alive after an HTTP request.
- Do not use PM2, Nginx or a permanently running Express server.
- Do not run background jobs using unreliable fire-and-forget promises.
- Use durable job records and scheduled processing for asynchronous operations.
- Use idempotency to prevent duplicate email and webhook deliveries.
- Respect Vercel function execution and payload limits.
- Use Vercel Cron or an appropriate durable job-processing service for scheduled work.
- Design for retries and partial failures.

