# 20. FIRESTORE DATABASE DESIGN

Use Firebase Firestore.

Design the database for a multi-tenant SaaS platform.

Suggested collection structure:

users/{userId}

organizations/{organizationId}

organizations/{organizationId}/members/{memberId}

organizations/{organizationId}/projects/{projectId}

organizations/{organizationId}/domains/{domainId}

organizations/{organizationId}/apiKeys/{apiKeyId}

organizations/{organizationId}/forms/{formId}

organizations/{organizationId}/forms/{formId}/fields/{fieldId}

organizations/{organizationId}/forms/{formId}/submissions/{submissionId}

organizations/{organizationId}/emailLogs/{emailLogId}

organizations/{organizationId}/newsletterLists/{listId}

organizations/{organizationId}/subscribers/{subscriberId}

organizations/{organizationId}/files/{fileId}

organizations/{organizationId}/webhooks/{webhookId}

organizations/{organizationId}/webhookDeliveries/{deliveryId}

organizations/{organizationId}/subscriptions/{subscriptionId}

organizations/{organizationId}/usageRecords/{usageRecordId}

organizations/{organizationId}/auditLogs/{auditLogId}

system/plans

system/settings

system/jobs

system/idempotencyKeys

Design appropriate:
- Document IDs
- Field types
- Timestamps
- Ownership references
- Query patterns
- Composite indexes
- Retention policies

Important:

- Never store all submissions inside one growing document.
- Use separate documents for submissions.
- Avoid unbounded arrays.
- Paginate large collections.
- Use server timestamps.
- Use Firestore transactions for critical updates.
- Use batched writes where appropriate.
- Prevent cross-organization data access.
- Minimize unnecessary reads and writes.
- Design counters to avoid excessive write contention.

Create and document the required Firestore indexes.

